      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B016"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B017. The lattice clock is a defined experimental method with a mature record of independent development, comparison, and improved precision. Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's central advances in realization support a focused two-person attribution. Its precision has enabled tests of fundamental physics as well as better timekeeping, giving the achievement lasting significance.",
    "case_against": "The history of optical frequency standards includes many contributors, and the boundary between foundational lattice-clock advances and later performance gains must be drawn carefully. The citation should recognize the clock achievement without attributing its full enabling infrastructure or every subsequent improvement to these two physicists.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors B001 for its pairing of the quantum spin Hall prediction with an early experimental realization, while noting that the boundary with the wider topological-insulator field and the group nature of the experiment complicate attribution.",
        "response": "That is a strong, mature case, and a citation limited to the quantum spin Hall effect would help define its scope. I still favor B017 because its repeated clock comparisons and independent development support a particularly clear experimental record for the focal method. B001's experimental credit also requires resolving how the decisive group contribution is represented by one named experimental laureate."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson ranked B017 seventh, recognizing its precision and maturity while questioning whether the prize should cover the original invention or broader advances in optical frequency standards.",
        "response": "I agree that the citation must be confined to optical lattice clocks. The method's mature performance and its demonstrated role in fundamental tests support recognizing the foundational clock advances, without claiming the wider standards field or later infrastructure."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes Katori's clock concept and Ye's complementary experimental advances, while calling for historical assessment of their relative weight and the boundary with enabling work on optical standards.",
        "response": "I agree. Their complementary contributions provide a coherent attribution, but a historical review should confirm which advances were decisive and keep enabling standards work outside the citation. Those questions warrant careful wording; they do not change my preferred configuration."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical record should establish the decisive contributions of Katori and Ye and distinguish them from enabling work and later advances in optical frequency standards."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
